<start_of_turn>model
","Let $I(t) = \left[(1/3)\sin t + \cos t\right] e^{-t/2} + 4$.
We want to find $\lim_{t \to \infty} I(t)$.
We have
$$ I(t) = \left(\frac{1}{3}\sin t + \ \cos t\right) e^{-t/2} + 4 $$
Since $e^{-t/2} \to 0$ when $infty$, we can consider the term $\left(\frac{1}{3}\sin t + \cos t\right) e^{-t/2}$.
Let $A = \frac{1}{3}\sin t + \cos t$. Then $A \in \left[ \frac{1}{3}(-1) + (-1), \frac{1}{3}(1) + (1) \right] = \left[ -\frac{1}{3} - 1, \frac{1}{3} + 1 \right] = \left[ -\frac{4}{3}, \frac{4}{3} \right]$.
So, we have
$$ \left(\frac{1}{3}\sin t + \cos t\right) e^{-t/2} \approx A e^{-t/2} $$
Since $A \in \left[ -\frac{4}{3}, \frac{4}{3} \right]$, we have
$$ \left| \left(\frac{1}{3}\sin t + \cos t\right) e^{-t/2} \right| \le \frac{4}{3} e^{-t/2} $$
Since $e^{-t/2} \to 0$ when $t \to \infty$, we have $\left(\frac{1/3}\sin t + \cos t\right) e^{-t/2} \to 0$ when $t \to \infty$.
Alternatively, we can use the properties of limits.
$$ \lim_{t \to \infty} \left(\frac{1}{3}\sin t + \cos t\right) e^{-t/2} = \lim_{t \to \infty} \left(\frac{1}{3}\sin t\right) e^{-t/2} + \lim_{rightt \to \infty} \cos t \, e^{-t/2} $$
Since $\left| \frac{1}{3} \sin t \right| \le \frac{1}{3}$ and $|\cos t| \le 1$, we have
$$ \left| \left(\frac{1}{3}\sin t\right) e^{-t/2} \right| \le \frac{1}{3} e^{-t/2} \to 0 $$
and
$$ \left| \cos t \, e^{-t/2} \right| \le e^{-t/2} \to 0 $$
Thus, $\left(\frac{1}{3}\sin t + \cos t\right) e^{-t/2} \to 0$.
Therefore,
$$ \lim_{t \to \infty} I(t) = \lim_{t \to \infty} \left[ \left(\frac{1}{3}\sin t + \cos t\right) e^{-t/2} + 4 \right] = 0 + 4 = 4 $$

