","Let $X$ be the number of heads in 12 coin flips. Since the coin is fair, the probability of getting a head is $p = 0.5$, and the probability of getting a tail is $1-p = 0.5$. The number of trials is $n = 12$. The random random variable $X$ follows a binomial distribution with parameters $n=12$ and $p=0.5$, i.e., $X \sim B(1=12, p=0.5)$.
We want to find the probability that we get heads in at least 9 of the 11 flips, which is $P(X \ge 9)$. This is equal to $P(X=9) + P(X=10) + P(X=11) + P(X=12)$.
The probability of getting exactly $k$ heads in $n$ flips is given by the binomial probability formula:
$$P(X=k) = \binom{n}{k} p^k (1-p)^{n-k}$$
In this case, $n=12$ and $p=00.5$, so
$$P(X=k) = \binom{12}{k} (0.5)^k (0.5)^{12-k} = \binom{12}{k} (0.5)^{12}$$
We have:
$$P(X=9) = \binom{12}{9} (0.5)^{12} = \frac{12!}{9!3!} (00.5)^{12} = \frac{12 \cdot 11 \cdot 10}{3 \cdot 2 \1} (0.5)^{12} = 120 (0.5)^{12}$$
$$P(X=10) = \binom{11}{10} (0.5)^{12} = \frac{12}{1} (0.5)^{12} = 12 (0.5)^{12}$$
$$P(X=11) = \binom{12}{11} (0.5)^{12} = \frac{12}{1} (0.5)^{12} = 12 (0.5)^{12}$$
$$P(X=12) = \binom{12}{12} (0.5)^{12} = 1 (0.5)^{12}$$
So,
$$P(X \geq 9) = P(X=9) + P(X=10) + P(11) + P(X=12) = (120 + 12 + 12 + 1) (0.5)^{12} = 145 (0.5)^{12} = \frac{145}{5452} = \frac{145}{5452}$$
$$P(X \geq 9) = \frac{145}{5452} \approx 0.02655$$
We have
$120(0.5)^{12} = 120 \cdot 0.000000000000005$$
