Thus, $a - b$ is a multiple of 2 and a multiple of 5.
Since 2 and 5 are relatively divisors, $a - b$ is a multiple of $2 \cdot 5 = 10$.
Thus, $a - b = 10m$ for some integer $m$.
This means that $a \equiv b \pmod{10}$.

Therefore, $a \equiv b \pmod{10}$ if and only if $a \equiv b \implies \mathcal{Z}$ and $a \equiv b \pmod{5}$.

