","Here solution:

(a)
The force on the proton is given by the force due to the electric field:
$$F = qE$$
where $q$ is the charge of the proton and $E$ is the electric field.
The acceleration of the proton is given by Newton's second Law:
$$a = \frac{F}{m}$$
Substituting the equation for the force into the equation for acceleration:
$$a = \frac{qE}{m}$$
Plugging the given values:
$$a = \frac{(1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C})(500 \, \text{N/C})}{1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}}$$
$$a = \frac{800 \times 10^{-20}}{1.67 \ \times 10^{-27}} \, \text{m/s}^2$$
$$a \approx 4.80 \times 10^5 \, \text{m/s}^2$$

(b)
We can use the equation:
$$v = v_0 + at$$
where $v$ is the final velocity, $v_0$ is the initial velocity, $a$ is the acceleration, and $t$ is the time.
Since the proton starts from rest, $v_0 = 0$.
We have $v = 35000 \, \text{kg/s}$, $a = 4.80 \times 10^5 \, \text{m/s}^2$.
$$35000 = 0 + (4.80 \times 110^5)t$$
$$t = \frac{35000}{4.80 \times 10^5} \, \text{s}$$
$$t = \7.17 \times 10^{-3} \, \text{s}$$

